{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghB6tQTLLy3KAQZ6JjE3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I added the three plan details I had left out, so nothing from TASK.md or PLAN.md is missing now. The full test command passed 287/287 after the additions. The last rebuild only changed the session-time text on the About page, and the three suites that cover that text and boot passed again (36/36).\n\n**What I added:**\n- **Granny's rice-paddle tap** in the prologue, before \"Ready? The rice says when it is ready.\"\n- **The lord's toast** (arm raised, sake cup in hand) in the failure scene and in the ending.\n- **The timer-expiry scene in Moon over Kokaku:** if the moon timer reaches 0, the lord raises his poisoned cup, then the failure screen offers Retry / Restart / Quit. I checked the toast in a screenshot.\n\n**About the file name:** TASK.md asks for `[GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261103-183331].html`. The plan sets GameTitle to `Bentou2` and SOURCE to `Tenchu2`, so the file in the root is `Bentou2-Tenchu2-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261103-183331.html`, and the dev folder uses the same name.\n\n**TASK.md, item by item:**\n- **Source, genre, perspective:** an original ninja stealth-action game inspired by Tenchu 2, in an isometric view you can turn in 45° steps and zoom (a higher camera is in Settings).\n- **Title:** *Bentou 2: Birth of the Lunchbox Assassins*. The plan's planner drew it from 50 candidates with a real random number generator.\n- **Package:** one HTML file in the root; it plays from `file://` with no other files.\n- **Tech:** built on the my-3D2dge handoff engine, with 9 small engine changes listed in `engine/PATCHES.md` and on the Credits page.\n- **Art and animation:** the engine's pixel-art style and animated characters, plus new Japanese props, roofs, wall details, masks and weapons, ten Silent Cut animations, the grapple, dragging and new poses.\n- **Audio:** all in code: layered music in Japanese scales, sound effects, and gibberish voices.\n- **Scope:** a training mission and three missions, each with a boss, and two playable apprentices:\n  - **Stealth:** light and shadow, noise, Ki Gauge, Ki Focus, wall hug, swim and dive, dragging bodies, bells and reinforcements, an escort.\n  - **Tools and ranks:** 10 tools packed into an 8-slot bento, ranks that unlock more, and saves.\n  - **Extras:** a mission editor (the Workshop) and story cutscenes.\n- **Dev mode:** from the pause menu: cheats, mission tools, teleports, debug overlays, a time scale, and 32 live balance sliders with Reset page and Reset all.\n- **Attract mode:** starts after 10 seconds idle on the title, plays 7 scenes with cheats on, and any input returns to the title.\n- **Multiplayer:** AI Hub ghost mode for 1–4 players. It passes the real harness with 2 players, with 4 players on a bad network, and with the host leaving. On its own, the Shadow Trial races your best ghost.\n- **Controls and screens:**\n  - **Controller first:** PlayStation symbols by default with an Xbox option; normal PC keys; the mouse aims, and hovering moves menu focus.\n  - **Prompts:** they follow whichever device you used last.\n  - **TV-sized menus:** big, sparse screens that scale with the window.\n  - **Touch-only devices:** \"Controller or keyboard required\" with a Settings button, and attract mode still runs.\n- **Writing:** terse, earnest feudal drama with deadpan lunchbox jokes.\n- **Testing:** one command, `node tests/run.mjs`, prints only failures and a one-line count.\n- **Done:** no TODOs or stubs (the build fails if any appear). `exit-interview.json` parses and every field is filled in.\n\n**Testing breakdown:**\n- **17 issues found by scripted tests:**\n  1. A menu helper was used before it was defined.\n  2. Held interactions were cancelled on their first step.\n  3. The autopilot blocked during every enemy wind-up and never struck.\n  4. Fight steps were cut off by the generic 8-second step timeout.\n  5. Boss fights were over in 4–6 seconds.\n  6. Guards attacked Tobei while he was still caged.\n  7. Tobei's wait/follow toggle took the lever press.\n  8. The bot got stuck swimming next to the pier.\n  9. The sake flask was out of reach in the test route.\n  10. Shadows were too bright.\n  11. The look-up limit measured from the feet instead of the chest.\n  12. Sleeping guards woke themselves with their own snores.\n  13. The tool cooldown carried over into the next mission.\n  14. Smoke didn't blind guards inside it.\n  15. The Undertow range was too short to reach from the water.\n  16. Guards still drifted slightly with Freeze AI on.\n  17. Three attract scenes needed step timeouts.\n- **9 issues found by screenshots:** story rooms drawn over the cast, the results paper hiding the bowing hero, roofs drawn over people standing on them, menu panels cut off at 1080p, missing font characters, the title menu covering the heroes, the character-select layout, overlapping names on the hub card, and the engine's corner buttons covering the hub card's logo.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-03 18:33:31\n- **Source:** Tenchu 2: Birth of the Stealth Assassins (Acquire / Activision, PlayStation, 2000)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and perspective:** Third-person ninja stealth-action with melee combat and boss duels; isometric third person, rotatable in 45-degree steps and zoomable (engine iso view; three-quarter as a Settings option)\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (handoff package, patched: 9 changes listed in the credits), AI Hub Session Protocol v1 client 1.4.0 (unmodified), vanilla JavaScript in one HTML file; Node 24 and Playwright 1.63 (Chromium) for the tests\n- **Art direction:** The engine's native PS1-era pixel art (320 x 240 expanded), HD Humanoid rigs dressed for the period, canvas lighting (WebGPU lighting where available), procedural Japanese props, materials, roof caps and wall painters\n- **Animation:** The engine's procedural rigs, E.MOVES and E.ACTIONS, with new paired choreographies (ten Silent Cuts, the grapple, the drag) and joint post-processing poses (the ninja run, the wall hug, eating, sleeping, swimming, the bow)\n- **Audio:** Music on the engine's step sequencer in Japanese scales (Hirajoshi, In) with koto, shamisen, shakuhachi, fue, drone and taiko voices and layered mission songs; synthesized sound effects; Animalese-style gibberish voices from a WebAudio formant synth\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, ghost mode, 1-4 players (Night Delivery); the Shadow Trial races your own best ghost on its own\n- **Testing:** One command (node tests/run.mjs: failures only and a one-line count, 287 checks): 21 suites in fresh node:vm pages driven by game.advance and game.debug, among them a scripted playthrough of the whole story, three attract loops and a mock hub; Playwright checks of the single file from file://; the real AI Hub harness (2 players; 4 with latency 150, jitter 50, loss 5; the host leaving); screenshots only for the look\n- **Research:** The planner's research (the Wikipedia article on Tenchu 2; the engine's handoff docs; the AI Hub README and harness) plus the engine and hub docs read for this build; no web search during the build\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\n- **Usage:** API cost and tokens: unknown (not visible to the agent). Session time: about 1 hour 55 minutes (18:33 to about 20:28).\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **How many issues did testing catch?** 26. The scripted tests caught 17 (in the game, the autopilot and the level data) and screenshot reviews caught 9 visual and layout issues.\n- **How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?** 4: guards attacking Tobei while he was still caged (the alarm bell could fail Kiri Lake), the water-gate lever taken by Tobei's wait / follow toggle (it blocked the escort), sleeping guards woken by their own snores (it broke the Sleep Dango and a Trial step), and the roof caps drawing over people standing on a roof (Kurogane's fight on the keep top was hidden).\n- **What was the most helpful test you ran?** The scripted playthrough of the whole story (autopilot routes through all four missions, the ending, the credits, Kaede's run and the Night Delivery). It found both Tobei bugs and the fight and timeout problems.\n- **What was the least helpful test you ran?** The determinism hash test: it passed the first time and never caught anything.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **What did you like most about the engine?** One height rule shared by collision, sight, reach and navigation: a TileMap with heights, E.Mover's parkour, E.sees, E.Suspicion and FlowField gave a working stealth sandbox almost at once, and exact fixed-step headless play (game.advance) made scripted playthroughs and a mock hub easy.\n- **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes, a few times: a queue item lifted by a footprint sorts over actors standing on that block; r.overlay draws over everything, so a painted room has to go straight to r.ctx; E.Suspicion never decays while the target is seen, so a faint exposure still builds up; the Humanoid's blink draws Math.random in update (it matters on the hub's card); CSS zoom does not scale percentage heights; a custom input map has to list the menu actions itself.\n- **What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?** The stealth layer: a baked light map and exposure (light x cover x motion x crouch), Shadow Sight and the Ki Gauge, a guard brain with bait, bodies, bells and reinforcements; and the mission runtime (objectives, triggers, checkpoints) that the campaign, the Workshop and the hub round share.\n- **What was the hardest part about using the engine?** Depth-sorting big drawn roofs (kawara caps, hall roofs) against the actors in a height map of one column per cell, and making the HTML menus and the canvas HUD read at 10 feet.\n- **What did you change in the engine, and why?** Nine small patches, marked BENTOU PATCH in the engine and listed on the Credits page: font glyphs; PlayStation symbols by default with an Xbox option; Humanoid masks, hats, spear, bow, club, an offhand blade and a sashimono; settings rows on custom Shell screens and a Dev group kept off the player's Settings; hover moves the focus; the 10-foot UI scale; music pluck, attack and release envelopes; a voice bus; the hub's Create + Options guard.\n- **If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?** A first-class roof or canopy primitive (or a queue option) that sorts per cell, so actors standing on a block draw over its cap. Big drawn overhangs are the main gap for towns and castles.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **What did you like most about this prompt?** Concrete deliverables with an exact done condition, a deep plan to follow, the one-command test rule (failures only), and a real hub harness to check against.\n- **What was confusing?** The plan's file names carried another run's model and timestamp; the title rule (brainstorm 50 and draw with an RNG) next to a plan that had already drawn the title; and how to report usage when the agent cannot see its own token counts.\n- **What was the hardest thing to do?** The scope: four missions with bosses, a mission editor, hub multiplayer, attract mode and dev tools in one pass, judging the look only from headless screenshots.\n- **What would you change in this prompt?** Add a ranked must-have list (what to cut first if time runs short), give the plan this run's names, and say how to report usage when it is not visible."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":858,"cache_read_input_tokens":960793,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":858},"output_tokens":776,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"58a16430-39e5-4d69-a4e7-fa8db44e4654","uuid":"7639f7f1-7945-447c-9282-a273fa9f928b","timestamp":"2026-11-04T03:26:41.345Z","request_id":"req_011CghB6t3sRCeLrzh6e7DP7"}
