{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghGbsmebpU5SkPg9WWqG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"These measurements are clean and come straight from the shipped scripts in the real mission. No game code changed while I built the report, so the final full run (368/368), the audit and the screenshots all still stand.\n\nThe game is finished. Per TASK.md line 28, no subagent review was used. The evidence below is what the shipped game itself reported while playing (`node tools/evidence.mjs`, saved in `tests/EVIDENCE.md`), tools written by others, and screenshots I viewed.\n\n**Correction:** last time I said the title appears on character select. It doesn't; that screen reads \"CHOOSE YOUR APPRENTICE\". The title \"BENTOU 2 / Birth of the Lunchbox Assassins\" is on the title screen (I took a fresh screenshot and viewed it), on the phone screen, and in the page's `<title>`.\n\n| TASK.md | Evidence |\n|---|---|\n| L3 Genre (stealth-action) | Measured at one of Rice and Ash's own guards, picked for the most light contrast in its view (see below). **In play:** Silent Cuts (back, undertow, above), poisoned bait eaten, detections, parries and ripostes, four bosses defeated. |\n| L5 Perspective | Q/E turn the view 45° each way. The engine's checker ran both views (iso, three-quarter) at 60 fps on every mission. |\n| L7 Title | Drawn with `secrets.randbelow(50)` (entry #27). Shown on the title screen, the phone screen and the page title. |\n| L11/L24 Package, dev folder | One `.html` in the root under TASK.md's `[GameTitle-SOURCE-…]` name. It loads no other file, and the dev folder has the same name. |\n| L13 Engine | The engine checker reports \"engine 0.85.2\". |\n| L15 Art | **Engine checker, all four missions:** no errors and no look notes against its PS1-era bar (65–144 colours, 84–94% filled). **Drawn:** 32 of the game's 34 own shapes (torii, hansho bell, shachihoko, yatai, jubako and more). **The cast:** jingasa ×10–11, kabuto with crests, sashimono banners, cloth and oni masks, spears, bows, an offhand blade. **The world:** 24 kawara roof caps in Rice and Ash. **Screenshots I viewed:** title, character select, market, keep, feast hall. |\n| L17 Animation | **Screenshots:** the Silent Cut grab, Gonroku's club wind-up, Granny's rice paddle. **In play:** every scripted cut plays to its end. |\n| L19 Audio | Voice lines play as at least one oscillator per syllable, and the music sequencer plays on oscillators. Nothing is decoded, no media element or speech synthesis is used, and nothing is fetched. |\n| L21/L44 Online, solo | **AI Hub harness:** passes with 2 players, with 4 on a lossy network, and when the host leaves. **On its own:** the Night Delivery is won and the Shadow Trial shows its board. |\n| L28/L38 Playthrough, scope | All six scripted runs win (teleports and god mode, counted in the report). Two characters, five missions, four bosses with 3–7 moves each, 10 tools, six enemy kinds, and the Workshop editor. |\n| L40 Dev mode | The real pause menu opens Dev Mode. 32 sliders, changed live by keys, and Reset page restores them. |\n| L42 Attract | Starts at 10 s idle and loops its 7 vignettes. Any key or mouse move exits it. |\n| L46 Tone | Lines quoted from the game: \"Lords devour lords, and the land devours the poor\"; \"Rice that is never tested is only seed\"; \"The law cannot reach him. Can your boxes?\" |\n| L49 Controls | **DualSense:** cross, the d-pad and circle drive the menus. **Keys:** WASD, Space, Shift, C, F, Esc. **Mouse:** hover moves the focus, clicks pick, a click in the world strikes. **Prompts:** follow the last device. **Xbox:** A B X Y is an option on Settings, tapped and switched in a test. |\n| L51 Screen density | Pause menu: 8 large buttons. A Dev Mode page: 8 sliders. About: one page at a time. Screenshots viewed. |\n| L53 Touch-only | \"Controller or keyboard required\" with a working SETTINGS button and no touch controls. Attract mode still runs. |\n| L56 Done | No TODO, FIXME or placeholder text in the source, and the demo plays through to the credits. |\n| L59/L75 About, exit interview | Every line of all six pages is drawn on screen and readable to its end. `exit-interview.json` parses with every field filled in. |\n\n**The stealth model, measured (guard \"gate2\" in Rice and Ash):**\n\n| Situation | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Standing in its lantern light, 50 px ahead | Alert after 1.7 s |\n| Crouched in its shadow, 86 px ahead | Suspicion 0.00 for 5 s |\n| Creeping 30 px behind it | No step noise; suspicion 0.00 |\n| Running 30 px behind it | Steps carry 60–72 px; alert after 0.6 s |\n| An alerted samurai | Rings the bell within 14 s (\"To the bell!\") |\n\n## Source Information\n\n- **Date:** 2026-11-03 18:33:31\n- **Source:** Tenchu 2: Birth of the Stealth Assassins (Acquire / Activision, PlayStation, 2000)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and perspective:** Third-person ninja stealth-action with melee combat and boss duels; isometric third person, rotatable in 45-degree steps and zoomable (engine iso view; three-quarter as a Settings option)\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (handoff package, patched: 9 changes listed in the credits), AI Hub Session Protocol v1 client 1.4.0 (unmodified), vanilla JavaScript in one HTML file; Node 24 and Playwright 1.63 (Chromium) for the tests\n- **Art direction:** The engine's native PS1-era pixel art (320 x 240 expanded), HD Humanoid rigs dressed for the period, canvas lighting (WebGPU lighting where available), procedural Japanese props, materials, roof caps and wall painters\n- **Animation:** The engine's procedural rigs, E.MOVES and E.ACTIONS, with new paired choreographies (ten Silent Cuts, the grapple, the drag) and joint post-processing poses (the ninja run, the wall hug, eating, sleeping, swimming, the bow)\n- **Audio:** Music on the engine's step sequencer in Japanese scales (Hirajoshi, In) with koto, shamisen, shakuhachi, fue, drone and taiko voices and layered mission songs; synthesized sound effects; Animalese-style gibberish voices from a WebAudio formant synth\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, ghost mode, 1-4 players (Night Delivery); the Shadow Trial races your own best ghost on its own\n- **Testing:** One command (node tests/run.mjs: failures only and a one-line count, 368 checks): 22 suites in fresh node:vm pages driven by game.advance and game.debug, among them a TASK.md suite with one check a requirement, a scripted playthrough of the whole story, three attract loops and a mock hub; Playwright checks of the single file from file:// through real keys, mouse and a fake DualSense (the About pages and the phone's notice and Settings read off the real canvas and page, the audio sources and every request counted); the real AI Hub harness (2 players; 4 with latency 150, jitter 50, loss 5; the host leaving); node tools/audit.mjs maps each TASK.md item to the checks that show it; node tools/evidence.mjs plays every mission on the shipped file's own scripts and prints what the game reports; the engine's own checker (handoff/tools/check.mjs): no errors; screenshots only for the look\n- **Research:** The planner's research (the Wikipedia article on Tenchu 2; the engine's handoff docs; the AI Hub README and harness) plus the engine and hub docs read for this build; no web search during the build\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\n- **Usage:** API cost and tokens: unknown (not visible to the agent). Session time: about 3 hours 15 minutes (18:33 to about 21:50).\n\n## Testing Feedback\n\n**How many issues did testing catch?**\n28. The scripted tests caught 18 (in the game, the autopilot, the level data and one in the engine's Settings screen) and screenshot reviews caught 10 visual and layout issues. Each is listed in tests/ISSUES.md with how it was found and fixed.\n\n**How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?**\n4: guards attacking Tobei while he was still caged (the alarm bell could fail Kiri Lake), the water-gate lever taken by Tobei's wait / follow toggle (it blocked the escort), sleeping guards woken by their own snores (it broke the Sleep Dango and a Trial step), and the roof caps drawing over people standing on a roof (Kurogane's fight on the keep top was hidden).\n\n**What was the most helpful test you ran?**\nThe scripted playthrough of the whole story (autopilot routes through all four missions, the ending, the credits, Kaede's run and the Night Delivery). Of the 78 failure lines the test command printed this session, it printed 37, more than four times any other suite, and it found 6 of the 28 issues (#2, #3 and #6 to #9 in tests/ISSUES.md), both Tobei bugs among them.\n\n**What was the least helpful test you ran?**\nThe determinism test (one check: the same inputs, played twice, give the same game): it never failed in any of the 60 and more test runs of the session, so it caught nothing.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n\n**What did you like most about the engine?**\nOne height rule shared by collision, sight, reach and navigation: a TileMap with heights, E.Mover's parkour, E.sees, E.Suspicion and FlowField gave a working stealth sandbox almost at once, and exact fixed-step headless play (game.advance) made scripted playthroughs and a mock hub easy.\n\n**Did you get stuck or guess wrong?**\nYes, a few times: a queue item lifted by a footprint sorts over actors standing on that block; r.overlay draws over everything, so a painted room has to go straight to r.ctx; E.Suspicion never decays while the target is seen, so a faint exposure still builds up; the Humanoid's blink draws Math.random in update (it matters on the hub's card); CSS zoom does not scale percentage heights; a custom input map has to list the menu actions itself.\n\n**What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?**\nThe stealth layer: a baked light map and exposure (light x cover x motion x crouch), Shadow Sight and the Ki Gauge, a guard brain with bait, bodies, bells and reinforcements; and the mission runtime (objectives, triggers, checkpoints) that the campaign, the Workshop and the hub round share.\n\n**What was the hardest part about using the engine?**\nDepth-sorting big drawn roofs (kawara caps, hall roofs) against the actors in a height map of one column per cell, and making the HTML menus and the canvas HUD read at 10 feet.\n\n**What did you change in the engine, and why?**\nNine small patches, marked BENTOU PATCH in the engine and listed on the Credits page: font glyphs; PlayStation symbols by default with an Xbox option; Humanoid masks, hats, spear, bow, club, an offhand blade and a sashimono; settings rows on custom Shell screens and a Dev group kept off the player's Settings; hover moves the focus; the 10-foot UI scale; music pluck, attack and release envelopes; a voice bus; the hub's Create + Options guard.\n\n**If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?**\nA first-class roof or canopy primitive (or a queue option) that sorts per cell, so actors standing on a block draw over its cap. Big drawn overhangs are the main gap for towns and castles.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n\n**What did you like most about this prompt?**\nConcrete deliverables with an exact done condition, a deep plan to follow, the one-command test rule (failures only), and a real hub harness to check against.\n\n**What was confusing?**\nThe plan's file names carried another run's model and timestamp, and a short GameTitle (Bentou2) beside TASK.md's own [GameTitle-SOURCE] rule; the title rule (brainstorm 50 and draw with an RNG) next to a plan that had already drawn the title; and how to report usage when the agent cannot see its own token counts.\n\n**What was the hardest thing to do?**\nThe scope: four missions with bosses, a mission editor, hub multiplayer, attract mode and dev tools in one pass, judging the look only from headless screenshots.\n\n**What would you change in this prompt?**\nAdd a ranked must-have list (what to cut first if time runs short), give the plan this run's names, and say how to report usage when it is not visible."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1660,"cache_read_input_tokens":359337,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1660},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"58a16430-39e5-4d69-a4e7-fa8db44e4654","uuid":"490c0bdb-877f-48ef-b17e-4fb6ebeefc39","timestamp":"2026-11-04T04:38:56.486Z","request_id":"req_011CghGbsGNzBnEMtthHmYuL"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says (TASK.md describes the game). Done when: the bundled single .html file exists in this folder's root, every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs, exit-interview.json in this folder's root parses as JSON and answers every field, and your final reply contains the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback sections.]: The file exists in the root and exit-interview.json parses with every field filled, and the final reply contains all four sections. But 'every feature in TASK.md is implemented' is not clearly established: the feature evidence is the agent's own audit mapping and self-written tests, with no independent verification. Some requirements rest on thin evidence (Art on one drawn-prop check plus screenshots; Genre on broad suites), and the agent concedes that no fully independent audit was possible. The condition is therefore not clearly satisfied."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"58a16430-39e5-4d69-a4e7-fa8db44e4654","uuid":"52b716bd-7da1-4931-b0d5-df5ceb0cf591","timestamp":"2026-11-04T04:38:58.015Z","isSynthetic":true}
